PCSX ReARMed (https://github.com/libretro/pcsx_rearmed, GNU General Public License version 2) runs the PlayStation, and
like every libretro core it says nothing of the instructions it runs. The tools apply this change of Dreamports' own to
its source before building it on the contributor's computer (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so the
program that plays the original (tools/libretro/host.c) can ask to be told of each instruction its interpreter is about
to run, and read the processor's registers and the console's memory then: that's how the function check takes its
memories of play, the memory each function starts from in play (tools/libretro/starts.h, tools/ps1/twin.mjs). Nothing
it runs changes: with nothing asked, each instruction costs one more test of a pointer.

